  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Where does my rubbish go?",
      "a": "A bin lorry takes it away. Depending on what it is and where you live, it is recycled, composted, burned to make energy, or buried in a landfill.",
      "asks": [
        "What happens to rubbish after it's collected?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens at a recycling centre?",
      "a": "Mixed recycling is sorted by machines and people into paper, metals, glass and plastics. Each material is pressed into bales and sold to factories that remake it.",
      "asks": [
        "What happens to my recycling?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How can burning rubbish make electricity?",
      "a": "In an energy-from-waste plant, rubbish is burned to heat water into steam. The steam spins a turbine, which turns a generator to make electricity.",
      "asks": [
        "What is energy from waste?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a landfill?",
      "a": "A big hole in the ground, lined with clay and plastic, where rubbish is buried in layers and covered with soil. It uses lots of land, and rotting waste gives off methane, a strong greenhouse gas.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is landfill bad?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How much rubbish does the world make?",
      "a": "About 2 billion tonnes of household and city waste a year, according to the World Bank, and the amount is growing.",
      "asks": [
        "How much waste do people throw away?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The World Bank estimated that about 2.01 billion tonnes of municipal solid waste were generated worldwide in 2016.",
    "Globally, only about one fifth of municipal waste is recycled or composted, according to the World Bank."
  ],
